  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The Athletics held a 4-1 lead through seven innings and lost it in two.",
    "what_this_means": "Late-inning collapse defined this loss more than any single moment. They built a lead early, held it through most of the game, and gave it away when it mattered most. Harris blew through a save chance in the eighth, serving up three runs in 0.1 innings. That inning turned the game. Elvis Alvarado followed with 1.2 scoreless to clean up the mess, but by then the lead was gone. Nick Kurtz drove in three runs to keep them alive. Two from a home run, one more on the ground. But seven hits and three strikeouts meant the offense never pulled away. They left runners in scoring position without capitalizing. Jack Perkins gave them five decent innings before the bullpen was forced to do heavy work. It wasn't enough.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what fragile looks like. The team has now lost two straight and fallen 1.5 games back. The ERA climbed to 5.00. Kurtz's production kept them in the game, but the timing mattered more than the production. He drove in runs that looked significant until the eighth inning made them footnotes. A one-run lead disappears when you cannot hold it in the late innings. They had the game. They didn't finish it.",
